WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.676
-Our next guest is

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:02.902
a talented actress you know

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.437
from films such as
"The Age of Innocence"

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.072
and the "Harry Potter"
franchise,

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:09.109
as well as the PBS series
"Call the Midwife."

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:12.078
Her memoir, "This Much Is True,"
is available now.

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.447
Please welcome to the show
Miriam Margolyes.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.248
Hello, Miriam!
How are you?

00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:18.685
-Hello, Mr. Meyers.
Nice to meet you.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.453
-It is lovely to meet you
as well.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:23.289
I want to admit that I have
sort of fallen in love with you,

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.824
not just for your work
in film and television,

00:00:25.824 --> 00:00:28.861
but I think you're one
of the finest talk show guests

00:00:28.861 --> 00:00:31.164
I've ever seen
on Graham Norton's show,

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:33.699
so I feel as though
you are on loan

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:36.502
from the Grand Norton Library
and we appreciate it.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:39.672
-Thank you for having me.
He's a delightful gentleman

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.507
and I always enjoy going
on his shows,

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:43.742
but I never know
what I'm going to say,

00:00:43.742 --> 00:00:46.712
and I've no idea what
I'm going to say on your show.

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.948
-We would expect
nothing less from you.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.583
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:55.054
So, now, this is a book
that you wrote last year

00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:56.555
when you were in Italy.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:00:59.492
And what made you decide,
at this point in your life,

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:02.361
to write a memoir like this?

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:05.531
-The main reason was because
the publishers approached me

00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:07.400
with a great deal of money.
-Oh, okay.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.002
[ Laughter ]
-And, when you get to 80,

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:12.571
you don't know whether you're
going to live much longer

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:15.775
and you might need carers,
and carers costs money,

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:19.712
so I thought it's
a good opportunity.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.981
And I never wanted
to write a book

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:25.752
because, if you tell the truth,
you hurt people

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.886
and I don't like hurting people.

00:01:27.886 --> 00:01:28.988
And I'm not interested

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.290
in celebrities
and talking gossip.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.759
That is not what I like.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:38.497
I like real life and that's
what I hope I put into my book.

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:43.035
But I put myself as well
because that was the deal.

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:47.540
And, so far, it seems popular,
selling well,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.975
which is what interests me

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:51.177
[ Laughter ]
and the publishers.

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:55.648
And I'm just astonished to be
on an American program.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.917
Because it's not on sale
in America, yet.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:03.522
It comes on sale in May,
when I will be 81.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:05.658
-Here's how not on sale it is.

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:06.926
This is a pretend book.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.461
This is not your actual book.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.029
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.797
-Alright, so I have
another question for you.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:14.967
Did you find it easy to write?

00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:16.636
-Let me tell you the way
we did it,

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.437
because it's rather clever.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:22.075
We did it via Zoom,
as we are doing now

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.577
and the publisher --
the editor, that is --

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.645
asked me lots and lots
of questions.

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:29.215
I did about 40 interviews.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:32.251
And then, they had it
transcribed by a typist.

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:36.389
They sent me the transcription
and I worked from that.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.924
So, nobody wrote it for me.

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:40.460
I wouldn't have
any ghostwriters.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.228
I want it to be
my authentic voice.

00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:45.097
And it is.

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:48.001
And that's how I did it,
because I can't remember things

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:51.103
that happened
40, 50, 60 years ago.

00:02:51.103 --> 00:02:52.638
You know, they will concertina.

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:54.540
You can't remember things.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.143
But I've had a full
and interesting life

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.612
and I've enjoyed it very much.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.614
As I said
in my introduction,

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:05.217
I've slept with a curious
variety of humans

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.119
-[ Laughs ]
-and that sort of thing's

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:09.889
always of interest.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:14.460
And I'm a voluble,
left-wing lesbian

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.262
and I just tell it like it is.

00:03:17.262 --> 00:03:19.265
That's what I want to do,
anyway.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.601
-I have read a tale
that you, maybe, were one

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:25.938
of the first people in England
to say a certain word

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:29.742
that you're not allowed
to say on television.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.944
-I regret to say that is true.

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:33.913
It was
in "University Challenge,"

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:38.251
a quiz show for students,
and I got an answer wrong.

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:41.453
And, of course, the expletive
popped out of my mouth,

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.789
as it would almost anybody's,
I think.

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:46.025
But, unfortunately,
the cameras were on me,

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.994
so it was a clear indication
that I was saying...

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:49.995
[ Mouths word ]
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:52.532
...and they beeped it out

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.367
and there it is.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:58.605
It's not a distinction
I'm fond of, but it did happen.

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:02.442
-Well, you've more than made up
for it in the years since.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.444
There's a wonderful story --

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:05.911
and there are
many wonderful stories

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:07.380
about people
you've met over the years,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.550
but you had an interaction
with the queen that --

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.084
-[ Laughs ]
-is it safe to say,

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:16.556
if the queen wrote a book,
you might not make it into hers?

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:20.192
-No, and I think she would be
right to omit me

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:22.027
because I was very silly.

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:23.395
It was British Book Week.

00:04:23.395 --> 00:04:26.398
Lots of people connected
with the book trade

00:04:26.398 --> 00:04:28.434
were invited
to Buckingham Palace.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:31.270
I was invited
because I record books

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.939
and I wanted to meet the queen

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.907
and I asked how you get
to see her,

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.042
because she was mingling
with the crowd,

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:39.011
and the equerry said,

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.948
"Well, you just form
a semi-circle and smile

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.016
and she'll, if she sees you,

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:48.120
she'll probably come towards you
and have a conversation."

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.024
So, it's not easy to form
a semi-circle on your own,

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:55.594
-[ Laughs ]
-even when you're
a rotund person, as I am.

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:58.797
But I found a friend
and we semi-circled

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:03.703
and we put our faces
into a rictus of a smile

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.571
-[ Laughs ]
-and Her Majesty saw us.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:08.641
And, amazingly, she came
towards me and she said,

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:12.578
as she often does, you know,
"And what do you do?"

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:16.248
And instead of saying,
"Ma'am, I record books,"

00:05:16.248 --> 00:05:20.486
I said --
I cringe as I tell you --

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:25.891
"Ma'am, I am the best reader
of stories in the whole world."

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:27.292
[ Laughter ]
And Her Majesty,

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:30.963
quite understandably, recoiled,

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.365
took a deep breath,
raised her eyes to heaven,

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:35.802
and smartly moved
to the next person.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:38.904
And she said to him,
"And what do you do?"

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:41.073
And he said,
"Ma'am, I record --"

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:43.209
Well, he wasn't a recorder,
actually.

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:45.645
He said,
"I teach children to read

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.547
and we found that,
if the letters are

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.949
in different colors, it absorbs,

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:52.784
the information is absorbed
by the children

00:05:52.784 --> 00:05:54.453
more quickly and easily."

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:56.689
I, standing next to him, said,

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.958
"My goodness!
How fantastic!

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:00.392
[ Laughter ]
I didn't know that."

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:04.530
And Her Majesty,
incensed at my interruption,

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.566
turned back to me and said,

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:07.967
"Be quiet."

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:10.202
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.538
The tea was very crisp.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.608
[ Laughter ]
And instead of being quiet,

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:18.210
I said, "Oh my goodness,
Your Majesty, I'm so sorry.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.079
I was carried away
with excitement."

00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:23.782
And she again raised her eyes
to heaven,

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:24.950
moved slightly away,

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.019
started talking
about something else.

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:30.556
I then interrupted her and said,
"You see, Your Majesty,

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:33.792
we're so lucky to have English
as our language.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:36.428
We have Dickens, Keats,
Shakespeare, Wordsworth."

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:39.165
And I went through the syllabus

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.199
and I could see,
from her expression,

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:44.703
it was slightly glazing over,
with terror, probably.

00:06:44.703 --> 00:06:47.006
I said, "Imagine, for example,
Your Majesty,

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:48.407
if we had been born --"

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:52.445
and I was searching for a nation
with a small literature

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:55.748
and I came across Albanian
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.985
and, at that,
she really did take fright,

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:01.487
clutched her handbag to her,

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:07.960
and tottered off, muttering,
"Yes, yes."

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.895
[ Laughter ]
Got away from me
as fast as she could.

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:13.599
So, I'm ashamed
of the incident, but,

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:15.168
now, I like telling the story

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:18.170
because I look
such a complete idiot.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.906
-Now, we don't have a queen
in America,

00:07:20.906 --> 00:07:24.143
but the closest thing we have
might be Dolly Parton,

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.212
who you also were
lucky enough to meet,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:29.215
I believe, with Graham Norton.

00:07:29.215 --> 00:07:31.516
-Yes.
I love Dolly Parton.

00:07:31.516 --> 00:07:35.287
I think she's absolutely
the best sort of American.

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:38.991
And I got -- because I was
in "Wicked" on Broadway --

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:41.894
the only time I've been
on Broadway was in "Wicked" --

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:43.963
and Joe Mantello was directing

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:47.133
and he directed her in something
and so, I asked him,

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.468
"Can you get me a seat
for her concert?"

00:07:49.468 --> 00:07:51.303
And he got me two seats.

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:54.006
I asked Graham to come with me
and we were --

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:56.541
we were going to meet her
afterwards.

00:07:56.541 --> 00:07:57.677
There's this --

00:07:57.677 --> 00:07:59.077
It's called meet and greet.

00:07:59.077 --> 00:08:01.914
You probably know that,
but I never heard of it before.

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:04.851
But, anyway, we were queuing
in the great tunnel

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:09.088
underneath Wembley Stadium
that leads to her dressing room

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:13.192
and I could feel this --
this fart

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:16.328
[ Laughter ]
building and crescendoing
inside me

00:08:16.328 --> 00:08:19.832
and I was holding on to it
as hard as I could.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:23.568
But, finally,
I just had to let it go.

00:08:23.568 --> 00:08:25.338
You know,
sometimes that happens.

00:08:25.338 --> 00:08:29.976
-Yeah.
-And I let it go.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:33.346
And it was the longest, loudest,

00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:37.984
not smelly,
but very powerful fart.

00:08:37.984 --> 00:08:39.485
[ Laughter ]

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:41.453
It stopped everybody
in their tracks.

00:08:41.453 --> 00:08:43.556
I was incredibly embarrassed.

00:08:43.556 --> 00:08:47.293
Graham was reeling in shock.

00:08:47.293 --> 00:08:50.495
But, eventually,
we got to see Dolly,

00:08:50.495 --> 00:08:52.197
and that was what counted.

00:08:52.197 --> 00:08:53.766
She didn't know about it.

00:08:53.766 --> 00:08:56.468
[ Laughter ]

00:08:56.468 --> 00:08:58.471
-Your book is filled
with wonderful

00:08:58.471 --> 00:09:01.573
and incredibly colorfully told
stories like this.

00:09:01.573 --> 00:09:03.776
Congratulations on writing it
and thank you so much

00:09:03.776 --> 00:09:05.444
for making time for us today,
Miriam.

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:06.678
-What a sweetie you are.

00:09:06.678 --> 00:09:08.014
Thanks for having me.

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:10.115
-"This Much Is True" is
on sale now

00:09:10.115 --> 00:09:11.917
wherever you buy your books,
but please support

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.352
local and independent
bookstores.

